proof_text:
We will show that we can choose an infinite sequence of nodes $t_0, t_1, t_2, \ldots$ of $T$ such that:
* $t_0$ is the root node;
* $t_{n+1}$ is a child of $t_n$;
* Each $t_n$ has infinitely many descendants.
Then the sequence $t_0, t_1, t_2, \ldots$ is such a branch of infinite length.
Since the root has infinitely many descendants, take its unique child $t_1$. More generally, take each node with infinitely many descendants to have exactly one child. That child must again have infinitely many descendants. Iterating this rule gives the sequence.
